ROLE: systems_reviewer  VOTE: green
PROMPT_VERSION: v001
EXISTING_IMPLEMENTATIONS: [
  {system: "Orca", url: "https://www.usenix.org/conference/osdi22/presentation/yu", overlap: "Defines iteration-level scheduling/continuous batching for generative transformer serving; directly covers cross-request decode-slot filling."},
  {system: "vLLM", url: "https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm", overlap: "README lists continuous batching of incoming requests plus PagedAttention; this is the packet's real incumbent baseline."},
  {system: "SGLang", url: "https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/blob/main/docs/index.rst", overlap: "Docs list continuous batching, paged attention, chunked prefill, and scheduler/runtime support; overlaps with serving mechanism, not agent-step-specific policy."},
  {system: "TensorRT-LLM", url: "https://nvidia.github.io/TensorRT-LLM/features/paged-attention-ifb-scheduler.html", overlap: "In-flight batching is described as continuous/iteration-level batching that interleaves context and generation requests to improve GPU use."},
  {system: "HuggingFace TGI", url: "https://github.com/huggingface/text-generation-inference", overlap: "README lists continuous batching of incoming requests for increased throughput; another shipping implementation of the incumbent."},
  {system: "LMCache", url: "https://github.com/LMCache/LMCache", overlap: "KV-cache sharing layer on vLLM; relevant to the packet's L1 caveat, but not evidence for agent-step-aware slot scheduling."},
  {system: "FlashInfer", url: "https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer", overlap: "Kernel library for prefill/decode/mixed batching and paged/ragged KV cache; not a request scheduler, so only indirect overlap."}
]
CONTRIBUTION_TYPE: insight
SCALE_REALISM: green for killing the slot-occupancy claim as stated: CB utilization equals the ready-work ceiling in all 288 configs, and artifacts exist with 289-line policy_compare.csv and 4321-line raw_runs.csv matching 288 configs and 4320 runs. Not sufficient as a real serving result: CPU-only model has 1 token/slot/step, fixed B, no prefill, no KV pressure, no GPU traces, and no real agent/tool latency trace. A later L1 must test vLLM/SGLang/TensorRT-LLM under KV admission, chunked prefill, and real tool-wait correlation.
RATIONALE: Shipping systems already implement the mechanism that the claim tried to beat: Orca iteration-level scheduling, vLLM/SGLang/TGI continuous batching, and TensorRT-LLM in-flight batching. The packet’s corrected paired-arrival experiment says residual_idle = ready-work-ceiling − CB decode-util = 0.000000 across all 288 configs, so there is no fillable slot idle for an agent-step-aware policy to reclaim in this abstraction. Step-aware vs CB shows median 0.00%, mean +0.32%, max +3.31% throughput delta, with only 7/288 CI-positive wins at no p99 loss and 66/288 throughput gains only by p99 sacrifice. This supports the negative systems insight: the proposed agent-step-aware slot scheduler is not a design advance beyond existing work-conserving continuous batching; any remaining plausible bottleneck is KV/prefill/admission, a different claim.
